{"connection_id": "BC-c92802bb73192409b9e8", "ref": "Didache 16:2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Available time and final usefulness", "note": "The community is told to gather often and seek what benefits souls because even the whole time of faith will not profit if one is not complete at the end; time, gain, and final perseverance converge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3301476f8131fb7f7a7c", "ref": "Didache 4:8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Mortal possessions shared with companions", "note": "The teaching asks believers who share in immortal things to share even more in mortal goods and not claim these as solely their own; the commentary's passing capital carries a mutual obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f81dc46c007f4a0bd99b", "ref": "Gregory the Great, Homilies on the Gospels 19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Vineyard hours as ages of life", "note": "Gregory reads the vineyard's laboring hours as stages of human life and the evening payment as final recompense; this makes explicit the commentary's move from a day's wage to an expiring lifetime."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acf54bb6e82f0bc9e80f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.58", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "steadfast work not in vain", "note": "The commentary couples labor with patience and a later wage; Paul urges steadfast, abundant work, saying labor in the Lord is not in vain. It assures the worker that present effort has a future result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae366c6f87d16e443b92", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.58", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Labor not in vain", "note": "Paul urges steadfastness and abundance in the Lord's work, knowing that labor is not empty; the commentary's loss versus enduring wage is stated directly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f794087b6d76a8516338", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Day exposes quality of work", "note": "Paul says the Day reveals each builder's work and tests what sort it is; the commentary's payday includes judgment of the work itself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ed40348a9c85b039091", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Enduring work receives reward", "note": "Work that survives the test brings its worker a reward; it supplies the positive balance opposite the loss in the adjoining verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d62d8d275253e3161d7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Work lost at reckoning", "note": "Paul says a builder whose work burns suffers loss though the person is saved; it distinguishes lost work from lost self in the commentary's final account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f9352bc515a5c4d51396", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wage according to labor", "note": "The commentary links work to the wage due; Paul says the planter and waterer are one and each will receive a wage according to labor. It directly connects effort to compensation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0bef803c5455d9de452a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wage according to labor", "note": "Paul says planter and waterer each receive wages according to their labor; this directly joins the commentary's intentional work and earned payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd9e1c00e3e6b4777b0a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Stewards must be trustworthy", "note": "The requirement for a steward is to be found faithful; it supplies a concrete office for the commentary's trust opposed to betrayal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2282e8a034f982cacb36", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.6.20", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "believers bought at a price", "note": "The commentary says God buys the believers' selves and promises a return; Paul says the community was bought at a price and therefore should glorify God in the body. The purchase image makes belonging entail action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f2d8be4d161d962818c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Paul's reading of the unmuzzled ox", "note": "The commentary says the worker has a right to the wage of the work; Paul reads Deuteronomy's rule against muzzling a threshing ox as written for human workers, so the plowman and thresher may share in the crop. This Christian interpretation extends the law's protection to laborers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc5195c0a46c0fa31ab4", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Foregone material right as reward", "note": "Paul asks what his wage is and answers that he preaches without charge rather than using his right in the gospel; refusing immediate pay itself becomes his chosen return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aec8e2488074a45f4f1f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.3.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Closed compassion despite goods", "note": "Someone with worldly resources sees a needy sibling and shuts compassion; it tests whether capital is used to meet the other's claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-51d7288108a92ebcbe53", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.3.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Love in deed and truth", "note": "The community is told to love in action and truth, rather than merely in words; it specifies how mutual commitment to truth becomes practiced good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a2e56b9e4c72d96c567", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ransom not paid in silver or gold", "note": "The commentary's divine purchase turns a person's life into capital; Peter says believers were ransomed from inherited futile conduct not with perishable silver or gold. It keeps the purchase image while changing both price and emphasis."}
{"connection_id": "BC-207448bcdff1999021eb", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.1.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work of faith, labor of love, endurance of hope", "note": "The commentary's exception combines belief, righteous work, and patient endurance; Paul recalls the Thessalonians' work of faith, labor of love, and steadfastness of hope. The triad closely joins the same kinds of action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc8338c13ed1acc602bd", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.1.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Work of faith and endurance of hope", "note": "Paul remembers the congregation's work produced by faith and endurance sustained by hope; faith, deed, and patient continuation are inseparable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b651cc013afa8afe3f54", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mutual correction and patience", "note": "The commentary says people urge one another toward what is right and toward patience; Paul tells the community to admonish the idle, encourage the fainthearted, help the weak, and be patient with all. It supplies concrete forms of reciprocal counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87cde3601d5a49c5e332", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Patiently admonish and help one another", "note": "The congregation is urged to admonish the idle, encourage the fainthearted, help the weak, and be patient with all; the counsel to patience takes concrete social form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87b737835d306f9ab7a7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.5.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "worker deserves wages", "note": "The commentary says the wage is owed when work is done; in instructions about elders, Paul cites the saying that the worker deserves his wages. It applies the laborer's due to support for community leaders."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc9254af88faf8050330", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.5.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Laborer worthy of wage", "note": "The letter cites the maxim that the laborer deserves wages in a discussion of support for elders; earned pay is an obligation of the beneficiary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-250d6be257d1c6afcec2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Uncertain riches versus living God", "note": "The rich are told not to hope in uncertain riches but in God; it corrects the illusion that one's holdings can secure a future."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cdadec1d8ea8eb5e6da0", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Good works as rich generosity", "note": "The wealthy are directed to be rich in good works and ready to share; it defines the alternate capital that the next verse stores for the future."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1431c7d70fb2acc28d67", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Stored foundation for coming age", "note": "The wealthy are urged to store a good foundation for the future and take hold of real life; the commentary's altered investment of wealth has a close analogue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8799eef1525ecc040c5f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Nothing brought in, nothing carried out", "note": "Paul observes that people enter the world with nothing and can take nothing away; the commentary's goods expire at the human boundary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1cd34574fab14d19be8", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Outer loss, inner renewal", "note": "The outer person wastes away while the inner is renewed daily; the commentary's continual expenditure of mortal capital need not entail loss of the person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f9db60aa28a6a8025d7f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Brief affliction, lasting weight", "note": "Paul says momentary suffering produces an eternal weight of glory beyond comparison; patient endurance yields a return not commensurable with the spent time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-da91facc95015041ca91", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Temporary seen, lasting unseen", "note": "The visible things are temporary and the unseen eternal; this supplies a specific axis for finite holdings versus enduring return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d9e06026765e5f976853", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.6.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "now is the day of salvation", "note": "The commentary warns that the final day has no market or chance to transact; Paul quotes Isaiah to say now is the acceptable time and now the day of salvation. It makes the present the open window for action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca621cf2d8e18fb91a4c", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.8.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "abundance supplies another's need", "note": "The commentary calls haqq each person's share and mutual recognition of another's due; Paul says one community's abundance should supply another's need so there may be equality. It turns right share into reciprocal material care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1780fa785b698b7fc3be", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.8.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Present surplus supplies another's need", "note": "Paul says current abundance should supply others' lack and later the relationship may reverse, creating equality; the commentary's mutual rights are an exchange across time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71c4233d9ae0482deca7", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sparing or generous sowing and reaping", "note": "The measure of what one sows determines the harvest in Paul's appeal for a collection; charity is treated as an investment whose return follows its measure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14ad1fe9f0ea0d8589be", "ref": "SBLGNT:2John.1.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Lose work or receive full reward", "note": "The readers are warned to watch themselves so their labor is not lost and they receive a full reward; it binds vigilance, loss, and complete payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ebdab63b4b4dc6188a5", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.3.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work and daily bread", "note": "The commentary includes manual labor and the wage earned by it; Paul tells the church that one unwilling to work should not eat. It expresses work as a condition of ordinary sustenance, though its context concerns idle members."}
{"connection_id": "BC-430df8874ffbbf50020b", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Finish the work, keep faith", "note": "Paul describes finishing his course and keeping faith; the commentary's worker must carry purposeful effort through to its endpoint."}
{"connection_id": "BC-214e947e8cbc0fcefdcb", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Crown allotted on that day", "note": "A crown of righteousness is laid up for Paul, to be awarded by the righteous judge on that day; payment comes after finishing the course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76c68ee30e41cf7cfffc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.10.30", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Ninth-hour prayer at home", "note": "Cornelius recalls praying in his house at the ninth hour; it shows the afternoon prayer time in another early Christian setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3da183456c6bd318faa1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.10.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Prayer and alms remembered", "note": "Cornelius is told his prayers and alms have come before God as a memorial; the afternoon prayer scene's charity is kept in a divine account."}

{"connection_id": "BC-b0de2034e59dc7caba9b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.35", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hand labor supports the weak", "note": "Paul says his work showed the need to help the weak and recalls that giving is more blessed than receiving; earned goods become another's benefit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccd86e66429378eff552", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ninth-hour prayer", "note": "The commentary names al-ʿaṣr as the afternoon prayer; Acts says Peter and John went to the temple at the ninth hour of prayer. The shared practice is an afternoon prayer rhythm, not an identical named rite."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e2594d7efd0b18919c2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Ninth-hour prayer", "note": "Peter and John go to the temple at the ninth hour, explicitly an hour of prayer; this is a concrete afternoon prayer beside the commentary's asr prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9a11b6cdaf66c321a062", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.2.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Good works still prepared for believers", "note": "The saved are described as created for good works in which to walk; it qualifies the previous gift language with a continuing place for intentional deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40ba901f92b2cfe688c3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.2.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Salvation described as grace through faith", "note": "The letter says salvation comes by grace through faith as God's gift; it puts a gift frame beside the commentary's wage frame for believing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4734024b4fa5316e493", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.2.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Not from works, so no boasting", "note": "The writer explicitly excludes works as the source of salvation; this differs from any reading of the commentary's good-work wage as earned entitlement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b35a4daf864572ee519", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Truth owed to fellow members", "note": "Each should speak truth with a neighbor because they belong to one another; the commentary's truth as an obligation owed across the community has a textual counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a69ac4fdd983cf9c2033", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.28", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Work honestly to share with the needy", "note": "The former thief must work with his hands at what is good so he has something to share with one in need; intentional labor becomes another person's due."}
{"connection_id": "BC-32eec690ba7d6d13a133", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do good while there is opportunity", "note": "As opportunity remains, the community should do good to everyone, especially the household of faith; the moral task has a limited open period."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e2790e0bdb5a058349f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Two investments, two harvests", "note": "Sowing to flesh yields decay while sowing to Spirit yields eternal life; the commentary's competing trades have concrete divergent returns."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7a151cd6a2d234de9ce", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "harvest in due season", "note": "The commentary's patient worker receives a wage after labor; Paul says not to grow weary in doing good, for a harvest will come in due season if one does not give up. It joins patient action to delayed return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3814ee42a99f03b5540e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Good work and harvest in due season", "note": "The community must not tire of doing good, because it will reap in due time if it does not give up; good action, patience, and deferred pay meet in one verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e25ce5c83faa814afa71", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stir one another to love and good works", "note": "The commentary makes mutual urging toward truth and patience part of the exception; Hebrews tells hearers to consider how to provoke one another to love and good works. It gives mutual exhortation an explicitly ethical aim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf8bd25cab27a1af70a0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Provoke one another to good deeds", "note": "The congregation must consider how to stir one another to love and good works; it is a direct communal form of the commentary's reciprocal exhortation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e08be4cd93339d1c939", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "encourage one another as the Day approaches", "note": "The commentary says the market closes on a coming day; Hebrews commands mutual encouragement all the more as the Day draws near. The approaching deadline intensifies communal action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e18f7be022b140be98a9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Encourage more as day draws near", "note": "The community must keep meeting and encourage one another increasingly as the Day approaches; reciprocal counsel intensifies toward a fixed deadline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e3f290d29609c07d8787", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.34", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Lost property and better lasting possession", "note": "The audience accepted the plunder of its goods because it knew it had a better and enduring possession; material principal can be lost without final loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a62eb62046b5942673fd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.35", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Confidence carries reward", "note": "The audience must not discard confidence because it has great recompense; the commentary's entrusted faith is sustained in view of pay."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ef5f8484f8dbbf8242f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance after doing God's will", "note": "The commentary joins patient endurance to a wage still to be received; Hebrews says endurance is needed after doing God's will to receive the promise. It makes waiting part of completed work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2625438135debacdb0f3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Endurance required for the promise", "note": "The audience needs endurance to do God's will and receive the promise; the time between work and due reward is filled by patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-793d183a676ab0f8d48a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God rewards those who seek him", "note": "The commentary makes belief one condition of escaping loss and describes a later wage; Hebrews says faith is necessary to approach God and that God rewards those who seek him. It joins faith to expected recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f677d28a779e35733f9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Belief in God as rewarder", "note": "The passage requires one approaching God to believe he exists and rewards seekers; trust and eventual payment are deliberately linked."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af0d41fede763c261885", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Esau's bargain read as moral warning", "note": "Hebrews recalls Esau selling his birthright for a single meal to warn against an unholy choice; it interprets Genesis's market exchange as sacrificing a durable inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f79ef06ba7adff109f13", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Blessing sought after the deadline", "note": "Hebrews says Esau later wanted the blessing but was rejected despite seeking it with tears; its reading of the birthright story meets the commentary's too-late plea for an extension."}
{"connection_id": "BC-045e2274c98de6b70183", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Exhort one another each day", "note": "The addressees must exhort one another daily while it is called today, lest anyone harden through deceit; mutual counsel has the commentary's passing-time urgency."}
{"connection_id": "BC-86834903e4f84f90ed14", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Work remembered by a just payer", "note": "God is not unjust to forget work and love shown in service to the saints; the wage due for good work is backed by divine justice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-160d2e95defc3dd9335a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith and patience inherit promises", "note": "The commentary pairs belief with patience and a future wage; Hebrews urges imitation of those who inherit promises through faith and patience. It makes endurance part of receiving what is promised."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb52b5a4228ffdc55d2a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Faith and patience inherit promise", "note": "The audience is told to imitate those who inherit promises through faith and patience; two terms of the surah's exception become conditions of deferred receipt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-75fbc52fe58f9ef2a3ad", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rich person fades amid pursuits", "note": "The scorching sun withers a flower, and the rich person similarly fades while pursuing business; time erodes both the trader and the expected gain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-61b79bf0f4d83f931ca7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Endurer receives crown of life", "note": "The person who remains steadfast under trial receives the promised crown of life; patience is tied to a deferred and nonmonetary wage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-373ed62b864a76db8834", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Brother lacking daily necessities", "note": "James imagines a sibling without clothing and daily food; the commentary's claims about good deeds and others' rights gain a concrete beneficiary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d717bc0a6ab147517bf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Words without material aid fail", "note": "Saying warm words without supplying a sibling's bodily needs is useless; mutual counsel must involve effective work rather than advice alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aee2e35cf19cd3bb8ced", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith without works is dead", "note": "The commentary names belief and righteous deeds together; James says faith without works is dead. The passage makes action integral to a living faith, rather than a separate supplement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f2c0f724355be4acc4c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Faith without deeds is dead", "note": "James says faith by itself is dead if it lacks works; it makes the commentary's paired believing and intentional good action a necessary conjunction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89ece901fe7c7ccada07", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Belief completed by deeds", "note": "James says Abraham's faith was active with his works and brought to completion through them; it develops the conjunction of believing and acting beyond the bare formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf1495ec8230fb3ed399", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Deeds and faith in justification", "note": "James concludes that a person is justified by works and not faith alone; it places the commentary's joined belief and work within a specific interpretive dispute."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d8a5918beb81370714f0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.4.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "merchants plan trade and profit", "note": "The commentary treats lifetime as capital spent in exchange; James addresses people planning to travel, trade, and make a profit for a year. The passage puts the trader's plan inside an uncertain lifespan."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09fcb0560a907a75cc3e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.4.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life as a passing mist", "note": "The commentary says the human being remains inside loss as time passes; James calls life a mist that appears briefly and vanishes. It gives the dwindling capital of time an explicit image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1b957ce75f9838916350", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "withheld harvest wages cry out", "note": "The commentary makes wage a due share; James says the wages withheld from field workers cry out and have reached the Lord of hosts. It links labor injustice to divine hearing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-416586b44140a860c08f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Withheld laborers' wages cry out", "note": "The wages kept from harvest workers cry out to the Lord of hosts; it specifies the wrong of failing to pay when work is done."}

{"connection_id": "BC-fb72d09b386ee8b1e52e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farmer waits for the crop", "note": "The commentary makes patience the condition of receiving a later wage; James compares patient endurance to a farmer waiting for the precious crop. It gives patience a concrete work-and-harvest scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bce18e0bc98904078290", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Farmer waits patiently for harvest", "note": "The addressees are told to be patient until the Lord's coming as a farmer waits for precious fruit; deferred yield and endurance meet in a work scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a44b70005c4aad4664f8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "patience because the Lord's coming is near", "note": "The commentary urges mutual patience as the end approaches; James tells hearers to be patient and establish their hearts because the Lord's coming is near. It binds endurance to a closing time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0067e48dad07a404df35", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Strengthen hearts, coming near", "note": "The audience must also be patient and firm because the Lord's coming is near; it makes the promised settlement, rather than waiting alone, the point."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e618465717e5638b3dd5", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Reaper receives wage for eternal harvest", "note": "The reaper already receives wages and gathers fruit for eternal life; the commentary's daily worker and final, lasting payment are joined explicitly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b029a48328879a99c2d1", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Labor for food that endures", "note": "Jesus contrasts labor for perishing food with food enduring to eternal life; the commentary likewise asks which expenditure of finite work survives."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f9490b6f3ebaeeefd8a", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.29", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Believing named God's work", "note": "Jesus calls believing in the one God sent the work of God; it ties the commentary's belief and work more tightly than a mere sequence of two acts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-466daf7cd2584e36f7ec", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.9.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work while it is day", "note": "The commentary sets the day worker's labor against the day's end; Jesus says his work must be done while it is day, since night is coming when no one can work. The saying makes the workday a figure for a closing opportunity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3331156618142e0a57ec", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.9.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Work while it is day", "note": "Jesus says work must be done during daylight because night comes when no one can work; this gives the commentary's closing workday a direct moral deadline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a96c67eefd586c3d0e36", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "laborer worthy of wages", "note": "The commentary treats wages as the worker's due; Jesus tells the traveling disciples that the laborer deserves his wages while they stay in a household. It applies the wage-right formula to religious workers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf1f9d52076a6991833b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Worker deserves pay", "note": "Jesus instructs envoys to accept a host's provision because the worker deserves wages; the commentary's wage is treated as a right, not a gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17c64c64c047e59a5ebd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich man expects many years of ease", "note": "The commentary rejects the idea that wealth makes a person outlive time; the rich fool tells his soul to rest because he has goods stored for many years. The speech exposes the assumed conversion of capital into extra life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f1ccba8dae1e975e8ab", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Stored goods imagined to buy many years", "note": "The rich man addresses his soul as having goods stored for many years and plans to rest; it states the precise illusion about wealth and lengthened life corrected in the following verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e6cb194847d39d0856ac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.20", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "soul required that night", "note": "The commentary says a market will close and a person may then ask for more time; God tells the rich fool his life is required that very night. It makes the deadline sudden and non-negotiable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-253726f30035e5383952", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.20", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rich fool's soul required tonight", "note": "The rich man's accumulated goods cannot extend the deadline when his life is required that night; this closely meets the commentary's hoarder who thinks property lengthens life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7f96366461624651f7b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.21", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich toward God rather than stored wealth", "note": "The commentary says amassed property cannot make its owner outlive time; Jesus concludes that the person who stores treasure for himself but is not rich toward God is a fool. It gives the alternative to temporal accumulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4694e2191c53d858363d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.21", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Stored wealth and poverty toward God", "note": "The parable concludes that storing for oneself leaves one poor toward God; it supplies the distinction between a full earthly account and lasting gain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c60d883c97fe0b36e237", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.33", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "selling goods to give alms and store unfailing treasure", "note": "The commentary presents a trade whose return does not fail and recalls a plea for time to give charity; Jesus tells hearers to sell possessions, give to the poor, and provide an unfailing heavenly treasure. It makes generosity a deliberate transfer from perishable wealth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba1f99b38c13cdad729b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.33", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sell goods for an unfailing purse", "note": "Jesus directs hearers to sell possessions and give alms, making a purse that does not grow old; diminishing earthly goods can yield lasting treasure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17baa5029ccd2cdc8de2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.14.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "repayment at the resurrection", "note": "The commentary links righteous deeds to a later wage; Jesus says a host who invites the poor, disabled, lame, and blind will be repaid at the resurrection of the righteous. It specifies generosity that cannot be repaid in the present market."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b4f37f22fb89e993120", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faithful or dishonest with little", "note": "The commentary glosses trust as the opposite of betrayal; Jesus says faithfulness in little predicts faithfulness in much, and dishonesty in little predicts dishonesty in much. The next verse applies this to money, making trust a test of stewardship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5116820b721a2aad0b92", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Trustworthy in small sums", "note": "The person faithful in very little is faithful in much; the commentary's trust in dealings applies to small transactions before a greater settlement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0b72fa0792a903a2d0f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faithfulness with money and true riches", "note": "The commentary sets trust against betrayal in a commercial setting; Jesus says that failure to be faithful with unrighteous wealth means no one will entrust true riches. It makes money management a test of trustworthiness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9c0b9773df948e200a29", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Trustworthy with unrighteous wealth", "note": "Jesus asks who will entrust true riches to those unfaithful with worldly wealth; handling temporary capital becomes a test of entrusted responsibility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a7ea95db85856dff60bd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Faithful with another's property", "note": "Jesus asks who will give the stewards their own if they have not been faithful with someone else's; entrusted capital is the test for lasting possession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-030b2cae455479a2fcc9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God and wealth as rival masters", "note": "The commentary frames the righteous trade as choosing the lasting good over a losing exchange; Jesus says a person cannot serve both God and mammon. It makes wealth a question of allegiance, not merely its amount."}
{"connection_id": "BC-769d81aa8c4c82c3664d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "steward called to account", "note": "The commentary makes business an account whose capital can be lost; the rich man in Jesus' parable demands an account from a steward accused of squandering his property. It makes stewardship answerable before the owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2a81d85b16d5332c7f1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.26", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fixed gulf after death", "note": "The commentary says the final day has no market in which to act or settle accounts; in the rich man and Lazarus story a fixed gulf prevents passage after death. It pictures the irreversibility of the closed opportunity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-38b91f5f1a1915515bf5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "use wealth for lasting dwellings", "note": "The commentary asks whether capital is spent on a durable return; Jesus tells disciples to use worldly wealth in a way that may welcome them into eternal dwellings. The link is a deliberate investment whose payoff exceeds the market."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c841849cc3f59d31536", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Use passing wealth for enduring welcome", "note": "Jesus says to make friends by means of unrighteous wealth so they receive one into eternal dwellings when it fails; finite capital can be spent for an enduring return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a0a19dd169cf678ae62", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.19.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trade until the master's return", "note": "The commentary makes the market open only for a limited time; the nobleman tells servants to conduct business with the entrusted money until he returns. The parable makes the interval before return a period of accountable investment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ccab04ff065e81a69c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.19.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Trade until the owner's return", "note": "A nobleman gives money to servants and orders them to do business until he comes back; the commentary's market stays open for a limited absence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3dd373bfd0e40cb5637a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.19.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "returns examined for gains", "note": "The commentary imagines an account closing after a period of work; on returning, the nobleman summons the servants to learn what each gained in trade. The scene makes the yield of entrusted capital reviewable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-938100b4736a8c27321c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.19.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Owner asks each trade's gain", "note": "At his return the owner summons the servants to learn what they gained by trading; it is a direct closing of the ledger after elapsed time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b98bb39a65a8d7da5ff0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.6.38", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Full measure poured back", "note": "The giver receives a good, pressed, overflowing measure, and the measure used is returned; it reworks the commentary's short measure into ample recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b561641f27087eca73d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.9.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Gain everything, lose oneself", "note": "Jesus asks what a person gains by winning the whole world but losing or forfeiting oneself; it is especially close to the commentary's gloss that the ultimate loss is the person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bab458cfd666268cfd5f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.8.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "profit of the world, loss of the self", "note": "The commentary says the ultimate loss is the person and family; Jesus asks what profit it is to gain the whole world and forfeit one's life. The saying puts market gain directly against losing the self."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c457cb2b79c33851f1f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.8.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Gain world lose soul", "note": "Jesus asks what it profits a person to gain the whole world and forfeit life; the commentary's losing capital ultimately means losing the self."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f9e9a0a6cee3c059184d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.46", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "merchant buys the pearl of great value", "note": "The commentary presents faith and righteous action as a better trade; Jesus' merchant sells all he has to buy one pearl of great value. The parable gives costly exchange a positive, kingdom-directed outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1674884158a722b3d9a0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.46", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Merchant buys one costly pearl", "note": "The merchant sells all he has to buy the uniquely valuable pearl; surrendering capital becomes a successful transaction, beside the commentary's saving trade."}

{"connection_id": "BC-138afbf6bcde5587dce2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.16.26", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Worldly gain cannot compensate a soul", "note": "Jesus asks what profit gaining the world has if one forfeits life and what could be given in exchange; it repeats the commentary's ultimate calculation of self-loss in another Gospel setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-284832f9cf5200eeb503", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.16.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Repayment according to conduct", "note": "The Son of Man will come in glory and repay each person according to what each has done; the self-loss question in the preceding verse is followed by the work account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-96f88d9cb57cb43c6217", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.19.21", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sell holdings and give poor for heavenly treasure", "note": "Jesus tells a wealthy man to sell his possessions and give to the poor to have treasure in heaven; another person's need converts current capital into enduring value."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e192f8838943b7d90f9b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.19.29", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Leaving family and property for life", "note": "Jesus promises a manifold return and eternal life to followers who leave family or fields for his name; it turns loss of family and holdings, disastrous in the commentary's quoted warning, into a chosen cost with promised return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-230250b990027107252c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "workers protest equal pay for unequal hours", "note": "The commentary speaks of the due wage for labor; the first-hired workers protest that the one-hour workers were made equal to them after they bore the day's heat. The complaint exposes the tension between measured effort and equal payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5df2284503fc57abfc68", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "One hour versus full day's labor", "note": "The late workers have worked only one hour, while others bore the whole day's burden; the contrast sharpens the commentary's question of time spent and recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb395d84ca7ed252bdb4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "agreed denarius as a worker's due", "note": "The commentary says a wage is an obligation once due; the vineyard owner answers a complaint by pointing to the agreed denarius. This gives the parable's wage a contractual basis alongside its generosity to later workers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-206b02bee6f5898b5805", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Agreed wage owed to the worker", "note": "The owner tells the complaining worker he has fulfilled the agreed denarius; this specifies the commentary's wage as a settled contractual right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0771e487023599635019", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "owner's generosity beyond proportional hours", "note": "The commentary presents work as earning a due wage, while also saying the patient receive reward beyond calculation; the vineyard owner gives the same denarius for one hour and a full day and defends his generosity. The parable presses gift beyond proportional labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c4e2865b195bd669500", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Owner's freedom over his goods", "note": "The owner appeals to his right to use his own money generously; the text complicates the commentary's strict wage ledger with unmeasured gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-472eac9984ac51601d90", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Agreed denarius for a day's work", "note": "The vineyard owner agrees with laborers on a denarius for the day before sending them out; it sets the contracted amount whose evening distribution drives the story."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc88b13ae38545024a59", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Eleventh-hour work opportunity", "note": "The owner still finds idle workers at the eleventh hour and asks why they stand there; meaningful work remains possible just before the daily deadline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d820f31b96f58965b98", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "workers paid at evening", "note": "The commentary's day laborer reaches the wage deadline at the day's end; in Jesus' vineyard parable the owner orders the workers called and paid when evening comes. The scene directly joins a day's labor to its wage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b2359f5815d7608d64f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Evening wage for vineyard workers", "note": "The commentary's afternoon laborer reaches payday as the day ends; the vineyard owner calls the workers at evening to receive wages, making the daily deadline concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4eb5d0edbe3860eff3ee", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.20.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Pay for eleventh-hour workers", "note": "The workers hired at the eleventh hour receive a denarius; payment after little remaining daylight complicates any simple equation of duration and wage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d9bc4bf274f663c4484", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Door closes while buyers are away", "note": "While the unprepared bridesmaids go to buy oil, the bridegroom arrives and the door shuts; the commentary's market closure becomes a concrete missed final opportunity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-765275b9bed2ae1121f4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Closed-door plea after buying", "note": "The late bridesmaids return and call to be let in; their failed return from the market echoes the commentary's plea for a brief extension once the term has ended."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bbf5192e9bb3a5fabb56", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Unknown day and hour require watch", "note": "The parable closes by telling hearers to watch because the day and hour are unknown; the closed door has an unknowable rather than negotiable deadline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b019b0cc41f60249ac19", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Servant trades entrusted talents", "note": "The first servant trades the master's five talents and makes five more; the commentary's believer as faithful trader has a concrete scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-158b61cc1f98ad32087b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "settling accounts after a long time", "note": "The commentary says capital is spent as time passes; in the talents parable the master returns after a long time and settles accounts with the servants entrusted with money. It links investment, delay, and reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-589c898e1fc7fb91d1a4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Master settles investment accounts", "note": "After a long time, the master returns to reckon with servants entrusted with talents; passing time and entrusted capital end in an account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fed6febdde8f041aa6fe", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.21", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faithful servant receives greater charge", "note": "The commentary connects faithful work to a wage; the master praises a servant faithful over a little, entrusts him with much, and invites him into joy. It adds increased responsibility as the reward for stewardship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b94ce98210e5613882d6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.21", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Faithful steward rewarded", "note": "The master calls the profitable servant good and faithful and sets him over more; profitable dealing is judged as trustworthiness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17eb443b90a2a6b5c664", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "money placed with bankers for interest", "note": "The commentary describes capital invested for gain; in the talents parable the master says the servant should have placed the money with bankers so it could return with interest. It makes the expected return explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a543e0dade6ce8a0ae27", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Capital should have earned interest", "note": "The master says the idle servant should have placed his money with bankers so it returned with interest; merely preserving the principal fails the investment test."}
{"connection_id": "BC-daf89d17a8c0f4bc8bc5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.40", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "service to the least counted as service to the king", "note": "The commentary says mutual counsel must recognize another person's due; in the judgment scene the king identifies care for the least of his siblings as care for himself. It gives acts of service a direct relation to the final judge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-688b829c09bcf541812b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.40", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Deeds toward least count toward king", "note": "The king treats care for the least of his brothers as done to himself; another person's due becomes part of the worker's final assessment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a5d75c63ef3054ac66fc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Oil sellers approached too late", "note": "The prepared bridesmaids tell the unprepared to buy oil from dealers; the concrete market action immediately before closure clarifies why the opportunity cannot be recovered."}
{"connection_id": "BC-073ca9f9b39198b48399", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure consumed by moth and rust", "note": "The commentary warns that what one owns is used up; Jesus says earthly treasure is vulnerable to moth, rust, and thieves. It supplies a concrete image of stored capital that cannot be secured."}
{"connection_id": "BC-77fed1874123732c00cb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Earthly treasure decays", "note": "The addressees must not store treasure where moth and rust destroy and thieves break in; this specifies how the commentary's earthly capital can disappear."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb4e8bdf5446284f000d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.20", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure that does not decay", "note": "Against the commentary's eroding principal, Jesus tells hearers to store treasure in heaven where moth and rust do not destroy. It redirects investment toward a durable return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1678ddef8cb2be7e164e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.20", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Treasure that does not decay", "note": "Treasure kept in heaven is beyond moth, rust, and thieves; it answers the commentary's contrast between exhausted human holdings and what endures with God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e311d897880236c93710", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "No hour added to life", "note": "Jesus asks who by anxiety can add to life's span; the hoarder's attempt to outlast time faces an explicit limit on extending life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c00cdebeb69d6ed02aac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "former gains counted as loss", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a losing trade from the profitable exchange of faith and deeds; Paul says what had been gain he now counts as loss because of Christ. The passage reverses the account's valuation, making apparent profit a loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-67c549538f4f371b42d1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Former gains counted loss", "note": "Paul counts what had been gains as loss for Christ; it gives the commentary's profit-and-loss vocabulary a deliberate reversal of value."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b85d569cbfdbddd470e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Lose everything to gain Christ", "note": "Paul accepts the loss of all things to gain Christ; the text distinguishes chosen material loss from the commentary's ruinous loss of self."}
{"connection_id": "BC-839f84716a7b83ede2f7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.4.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Gift produces credit in the giver's account", "note": "Paul says he seeks fruit increasing to the donors' account rather than the gift itself; generosity becomes an accumulating return in the commentary's moral ledger."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2bf55e6444c13fe92caa", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phlm.1.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "another person stands behind a debt", "note": "The commentary's lexical branch calls sabīr a guarantor; Paul tells Philemon to charge any debt or wrong by Onesimus to Paul's own account. It gives the guarantor image a concrete personal act of bearing another's liability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18a6fba6eb29a282c1ad", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rest from labors, deeds follow", "note": "The dead in the Lord rest from labor and their deeds follow them; the wage scene acquires a precise boundary between ended labor and surviving deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d5e89713bf6357eb707", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.18.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Merchants grieve because trade stops", "note": "The earth's merchants mourn because no one buys their cargo anymore; the commentary's open market ends without another buyer."}

{"connection_id": "BC-ccd62c945bd550a3ef8d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.18.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wealth ruined in one hour", "note": "The lament says great wealth is laid waste in a single hour; it compresses the commentary's continuing capital loss into a final moment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e306bf4b0c975a879c3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faithful through death, crowned with life", "note": "The commentary couples patience with a wage at the end of the worker's time; Revelation tells Smyrna to remain faithful even to death and promises the crown of life. It makes endurance under threat part of the reward image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cda7c2327ec33ed3b4a2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Faith, service, endurance, growing works", "note": "The speaker knows this church's works, faith, service, and endurance, and says its later works exceed earlier ones; the surah's exception is matched in a timed communal assessment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-08d59eb7e9a1f99cb6ec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.23", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Heart-searcher gives by works", "note": "The speaker says he searches minds and hearts and will give each according to works; intentional acts and the final payout are assessed together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa1c00a94044cf69a30f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.26", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Keep works to the end", "note": "The one who conquers and keeps the speaker's works until the end receives authority; the reward requires continuing good action across the remaining time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b994ce6d4af20902b764", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "reward according to each person's work", "note": "The commentary presents a wage arriving after a period of labor; the returning Christ says he brings recompense to repay each according to work. It makes the final wage explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4b52f78a0d9786103e1e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Coming with recompense for work", "note": "The coming speaker brings payment to repay each according to work; it matches the commentary's final rather than immediate settlement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a95ad5a1bcb6767060ea", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.3.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Buy refined gold from the speaker", "note": "The speaker counsels a materially self-satisfied church to buy gold from him so it becomes rich; true capital is defined against apparent wealth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5a1739c4b628590287e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.5.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "people purchased for God by blood", "note": "The commentary pictures God as buyer of believers' lives; the heavenly song says the slain one purchased people for God by his blood. The image parallels redemption as costly acquisition while naming a different purchaser's price."}
{"connection_id": "BC-95490f487feba337c1d5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.1.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Paul's reception of Habakkuk's faithfulness", "note": "The commentary makes belief a condition for escaping loss; Paul quotes Habakkuk's righteous one living by faith in his account of the gospel's revealed righteousness. This Christian reading shifts the verse toward faith in the gospel from Habakkuk's faithful endurance under threat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e854e73e4ccf45e821c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Each repaid according to deeds", "note": "God repays every person according to deeds; the commentary's final wage uses an individual account of actual work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2e6cb4d8aa41661b9dc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Persistence in good work seeks immortality", "note": "People who persist in good action seek glory and immortality and receive eternal life; patient work answers the hoarder's false bid for immortality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-915959cb7c196d752fae", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.4.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wage counted as debt, not gift", "note": "The commentary says a worker's wage is due and uses the language of a right; Paul says a worker's wage is reckoned as debt rather than grace. His argument about justification makes the earned-wage analogy precise while distinguishing it from a gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea1e231c43f9dcc35cc3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.4.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Wage due versus gracious gift", "note": "Paul distinguishes a worker's pay credited as an obligation from a gift credited by grace; this tests the commentary's moral wage metaphor against an explicit refusal to make divine credit ordinary wages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfe495fb89ac42892ce5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.4.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Belief credited without work", "note": "Paul says the one who does not work but trusts God is credited with righteousness; the passage presses against reading belief and good work as jointly earning the payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-765d94f0a10830ccedcc", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Bava Metzia 83a", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "porters' wages and confiscated cloaks", "note": "The commentary treats a laborer's wage as a right; in this story an employer takes porters' cloaks after they break barrels, and Rav orders him to return the cloaks and pay them. The case gives worker rights priority over the employer's loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-846c1e33ea547522bec5", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 31a", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ء م ن; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew/Aramaic אמנ", "note": "The commentary glosses amānah as trust opposed to betrayal; at judgment the Talmud asks whether a person conducted business faithfully. This joins trust to commerce in the same concrete setting, though cognacy is unverified."}
