{"connection_id": "BC-76fa642e80210432aab8", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 2:1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Calculate loss of a good deed against reward", "note": "The saying instructs a person to reckon a commandment's apparent loss against its reward and a transgression's apparent gain against its loss; it articulates the commentary's moral profit calculation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6783658358a468968ab0", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 2:15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Day short, work great, wage great", "note": "Rabbi Tarfon's saying calls the day short, work great, workers sluggish, and reward abundant; the commentary's entire daily labor image is compressed here."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b8974c9d021619188055", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 2:16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Trustworthy employer pays worker", "note": "The continuation says the employer is trustworthy to pay for labor and places the righteous person's reward in the future; it joins amanah, wage, and delayed payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c22cea7ec8b63a187476", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Open shop and written ledger", "note": "The parable's shop extends credit while a written ledger and collectors bring every debt to account; it mirrors the commentary's open market and mutual obligations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79d465aa8cc91c0dfd2f", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Prepare in vestibule before entering", "note": "The present life is a vestibule where one prepares for the coming hall; finite preparatory time precedes the lasting result of work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3bd2221401028f8e5955", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "One hour of deeds before the next world", "note": "The saying values one hour of repentance and good deeds in this life above the whole coming life, giving a sharp reason to use remaining time now."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3872fcd1a9c8919cd475", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The grave does not cancel accounting", "note": "The saying warns that death cannot serve as a refuge because one must give an account before the divine king; the commentary's closing day still leaves an audit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-265423f26f0ceef2534f", "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 4:1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Afternoon prayer's deadline", "note": "The mishnah assigns a latest time to the afternoon prayer, with a reported disagreement over its extent; it contextualizes the commentary's named afternoon prayer as timed practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb55504c20c1f7c63308", "ref": "Mishnah, Avot 1:17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "action over study alone", "note": "The commentary's exception requires belief expressed through righteous deeds; Avot says action is the essential thing, not study alone. It gives Jewish ethical weight to the move from conviction to practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39eeb3a1657b1db48896", "ref": "Mishnah, Avot 2:15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "short day, abundant work, great reward", "note": "The commentary's human life is capital spent in work and paid at day's end; Rabbi Tarfon says the day is short, the work abundant, and the reward great. The saying compresses the commentary's time-and-wage image into one maxim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9c3329120aeb29227818", "ref": "Mishnah, Avot 3:16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "open shop, ledger, daily collectors", "note": "The commentary imagines time as capital and a final account; Avot depicts the world as an open shop with credit, a ledger, and collectors making daily rounds. It turns daily life into a debt that will be collected."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1becfafc9c122d1e7e46", "ref": "Mishnah, Avot 4:17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one hour for deeds in this world", "note": "The commentary insists that work must be done before the market closes; Avot says one hour of repentance and good deeds in this world outweighs the whole life of the world to come. It makes present time the irreplaceable arena of action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0074047e46b1f6532496", "ref": "Sirach 11:19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wealth cannot secure one's remaining time", "note": "The commentary rejects the belief that amassed wealth can outlast time; Sirach describes a rich person who plans to enjoy his goods but does not know how soon he will die and leave them. It directly joins capital to a life one cannot extend."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34e785b7c474bc0f24a6", "ref": "Sirach 14:12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Remember death's undelayed claim", "note": "The instruction says to remember that death will not delay; it counters the commentary's hope that a short extension will remain available."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39fe9307f580c048cfd8", "ref": "Sirach 14:13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Give to a friend before death", "note": "The speaker urges doing good to a friend and giving while still alive; possession can meet another's claim only before the human deadline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d733a34abe270547dca", "ref": "Sirach 29:11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Alms as secured treasure", "note": "The speaker urges placing one's treasure according to the Most High's commandments and promises more profit than gold; moral expenditure is a safer investment than hoarding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-036d7e90f43f658ac420", "ref": "Sirach 29:14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Neighbor's guarantor", "note": "The instruction praises giving security for a neighbor; it supplies a Jewish debt scene for the source commentary's secondary sense of sabir as a guarantor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-94ad9fea841f8cbe9503", "ref": "Sirach 29:15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Guarantor risks his own life", "note": "The borrower is told not to forget a guarantor who has given his life for him; the source's secondary sabir as guarantor becomes a concrete and costly interpersonal obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e0e803d87adb2594e8b9", "ref": "Sirach 5:7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do not delay turning back", "note": "The commentary ends with a person asking for a little more time to give and do right; Sirach warns not to delay turning to the Lord, since judgment can come suddenly. It makes postponement itself a danger."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6b3e91a929f7bf319b5", "ref": "Tobit 12:8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Little with righteousness exceeds unjust wealth", "note": "Raphael ranks prayer and almsgiving with righteousness above riches with injustice; the scale of moral value differs from the amount in a purse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f2dde0cb6c7aa588d39", "ref": "Tobit 4:10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Alms rescue from death", "note": "Tobit tells his son almsgiving delivers from death and prevents descent into darkness; the stored treasure in the prior instruction has a concrete life-saving return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1347703928e7b5e98320", "ref": "Tobit 4:9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "alms stored as treasure for a future day", "note": "The commentary contrasts wealth that perishes with a trade that does not fail; Tobit says alms lay up a good treasure for the day of need. It makes generosity a future-oriented deposit."}

{"connection_id": "BC-b8e6b764b92699aba14f", "ref": "Tobit 4:9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Alms stored as future treasure", "note": "Tobit tells his son that almsgiving stores up good treasure for a day of need; the commentary's finite possessions can become a lasting credit through another's right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b04a2002956802a5573", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.20.20", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ and Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח, table sound correspondences only", "note": "Jehoshaphat tells the people to believe the Lord and be established, and believe his prophets and succeed; the verse puts two table roots in sequence, linking trust to a prospering outcome without proving either cognacy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ee1bd5846c315d9f053", "ref": "WLC:Amos.8.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "false ephah and heavy shekel", "note": "The commentary's underweight scale has a close market scene here: sellers shrink the measure, enlarge the shekel, and wait for the Sabbath to end. Amos exposes fraud hidden behind ordinary trade."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74c728f4527c8c85921b", "ref": "WLC:Amos.8.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Shrink measure, enlarge price, falsify scales", "note": "Merchants impatient for sacred time to end make the grain measure small and the price large; market hours and short measure meet exactly as in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20e6456800525c35cf0c", "ref": "WLC:Amos.8.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "buying the poor for silver", "note": "The commentary treats a person's due share as a debt; Amos's merchants want to acquire the poor for silver and the needy for sandals. The passage shows false trade turning into human exploitation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7987fbf69b4392541c29", "ref": "WLC:Amos.8.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "selling grain refuse as good grain", "note": "The commentary describes a market where the scale makes a short measure; Amos's sellers wait to sell even the refuse of grain. This adds deception about quality to the commentary's image of deception by weight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-622a6e49838dd56ab9da", "ref": "WLC:Amos.8.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Buying the poor for silver", "note": "The cheating merchants then buy the needy for silver; the crooked market's loss reaches a human person, not merely a measure of grain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ddbbac25331444ecbd9", "ref": "WLC:Dan.5.26", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Numbered kingdom brought to an end", "note": "Daniel explains that God has numbered the king's days and ended his rule; the counted time immediately precedes the short weight in the next verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4d0d0f36a877c70780a", "ref": "WLC:Dan.5.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic خ س ر / Aramaic חסר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The king is weighed in the balances and found deficient; the table's Aramaic lacking root joins a literal scale to the commentary's moral shortfall, though cognacy remains unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d09b6f3e7c094287092", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.29", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Aramaic צלח, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Daniel prospers under Darius and Cyrus after surviving a trial of fidelity; the Aramaic prosperity verb is a cautious sound link to the commentary's salih outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-82a31ae9875669ab34dc", "ref": "WLC:Deut.15.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Give without grief, blessing on work", "note": "The giver is told to give freely, with God's blessing promised on work; another's material due and the worker's return are joined."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bbe5c7ffe618a114ec5e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.15.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root خ س ر; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew חסר", "note": "The commentary defines loss through diminution and deficiency; Deuteronomy commands opening a hand to a poor person for whatever he lacks (חסר). It connects lack to a neighbor's material need, though cognacy is unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-38ff49c4f000b3ca2d3e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.15.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Open hand and sufficient loan", "note": "The command is to open the hand and lend a needy brother enough for his need; the commentary's mutual debt becomes a specified financial duty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b8806c200e2c61374d4a", "ref": "WLC:Deut.15.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do not delay aid near debt release", "note": "The lender is warned not to refuse a needy brother merely because the seventh year is near; the debt deadline must not cancel the other's immediate due."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c2a0cd795766aa15a92", "ref": "WLC:Deut.16.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Arabic ع ص ر / Hebrew עצר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The final festival day is called a solemn assembly and ordinary work stops; the table's Hebrew root may suggest a bounded communal time beside the commentary's afternoon prayer and ended work, though its restraint sense is different from the developed Arabic time sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2f78007fc9dc1f72777", "ref": "WLC:Deut.24.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "oppressing the poor hired worker", "note": "The commentary treats work as labor that earns a right; Deuteronomy forbids oppressing a needy hired worker, whether Israelite or resident foreigner. It extends wage justice beyond one's own kin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-218b434a6611ad19d7ab", "ref": "WLC:Deut.24.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do not exploit a needy hired worker", "note": "The rule forbids oppression of poor hired workers, including resident foreigners; it identifies who is vulnerable before the same-day wage rule in the next verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e128569a70977c0b5787", "ref": "WLC:Deut.24.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pay before sunset", "note": "The commentary's day laborer reaches the wage deadline at day's end; Deuteronomy requires payment before sunset because the worker depends on it, and says the worker may cry to God. It makes delay a matter of life and justice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b7830252a2cf69c1200", "ref": "WLC:Deut.24.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wages before sunset", "note": "The hired poor worker's wage must be paid that day before the sun sets, providing a legal counterpart to the commentary's afternoon and due wage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-294df3bfc89590f17fea", "ref": "WLC:Deut.24.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gleanings left for the vulnerable", "note": "The commentary calls haqq a person's due share; Deuteronomy requires a forgotten sheaf to remain for the foreigner, orphan, and widow. It makes the vulnerable person's claim visible in ordinary harvest work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d0ca4cb661b5c03d9e9a", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Different weights in one bag", "note": "The prohibition on large and small weights describes the exact equipment for the commentary's cheating scale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e590525a534246a26c13", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "full weight and long days", "note": "The commentary sets the loss of capital against time running out; Deuteronomy links a full, just weight with long life in the land. Honest trade sustains communal life across time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e0c8acf38613cdf7565", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Full measure prolongs days", "note": "The demand for a complete and just weight is attached to prolonged days in the land; measured fairness and the duration of life intersect."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be19f3817a76e3584614", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do not muzzle the threshing ox", "note": "The commentary includes work done by hands and the wage due for labor; Deuteronomy forbids muzzling an ox while it threshes. The law recognizes a worker's right to benefit while producing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09dde29cc409e45b956d", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ء م ن; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew אמנ", "note": "The commentary glosses amānah as trust opposed to betrayal; Deuteronomy calls God faithful and without injustice. This Hebrew use joins fidelity to justice, though the root correspondence remains unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3b6f0e07d9380abffaa", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.1.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic خ س ر / Hebrew חסר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "What is lacking cannot be counted, the speaker says; the Hebrew deficiency noun makes the commentary's arithmetic of loss explicit, though the proposed root relation remains unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c8478da29ea1b199113", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.1.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Profit from toil under the sun", "note": "Ecclesiastes asks what gain people have from all the toil they perform under the sun; the same accounting question frames life as spent effort."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d0c24d050b018440e208", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bread invested for later return", "note": "The speaker urges sending bread on the waters because it will be found after many days; it images an expenditure whose return waits beyond the present."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17723c66cac8888dcece", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sow morning and evening", "note": "In the morning one sows, and at evening one must not let the hand rest, since one cannot know which work succeeds; action continues up to the day's boundary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d8ecb411aabf9862f2d", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember the creator before difficult days", "note": "The commentary says a late plea for more time comes after the market has closed; Ecclesiastes urges remembering the creator in youth before the difficult days arrive. It makes the timing of good action urgent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-00ea59d378a6ccc48290", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Every work called to judgment", "note": "God will bring every deed, even hidden ones, to judgment; the commentary's final pay is based on a complete account of work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-651dd925b1ba52b9215e", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.2.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Works inspected, no gain", "note": "The speaker surveys all the work his hands made and finds no lasting profit under the sun; it is a concrete contrary ledger to profitable moral work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ec4cbfb2f64a09c1434e", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.2.21", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع م ل / Hebrew עמל, table sound correspondence only", "note": "One who toils with wisdom and skill may leave the result to someone who has not toiled; the Hebrew labor root asks who ultimately gets the worker's earned portion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-066f34ad9b4c30f02983", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.2.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gain from toil under the sun", "note": "The commentary makes time the capital spent in work; Ecclesiastes asks what a person gains from all toil and anxious effort under the sun. The question sharpens the uncertainty of labor's return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a93f677940c32764df3a", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.2.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ع م ل; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew עמל", "note": "The commentary labels ʿamal as intentional work, manual labor, and its wage; Ecclesiastes uses עמל for toil and asks its gain. The shared labor term links work to uncertain return, though the table does not verify cognacy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dfe32a6b2c304c5e7d7f", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.5.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wealth lost in a bad venture", "note": "Ecclesiastes describes riches lost through a bad business affair, leaving nothing for a child; the loss of principal and family consequence are both present."}
{"connection_id": "BC-473d037ac7ac4b71eeee", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.5.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wealth lost in a bad venture", "note": "The commentary describes trade consuming its principal; Ecclesiastes says wealth can be lost in a bad venture, leaving a father with nothing to pass on. It gives the loss of capital a concrete household consequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9400eb0425e920d428b5", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.5.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "No earnings carried out of life", "note": "A person leaves this life as naked as at birth and carries nothing gained by toil; the commentary's trader cannot take the day's proceeds past death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-effbe130507da7431221", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.5.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nothing carried away from toil", "note": "The commentary says accumulated wealth cannot outlast a person's time; Ecclesiastes says a person leaves as naked as at birth and takes nothing from toil. It makes the final loss bodily and irreversible."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09d7fd198f2239c97d95", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.9.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work before death", "note": "The commentary says the market will close and the worker's day will end; Ecclesiastes urges doing with might what one's hand finds, because there is no work in Sheol. It makes finite time the condition for action."}

{"connection_id": "BC-82a6a021539c36cde5e5", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.9.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do the work before death", "note": "One must do with strength whatever the hand finds because no work or planning is done in Sheol; it makes a finite work period ethically urgent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c371bb27dfc6e8ee6f6f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shared support keeps hands steady until sunset", "note": "The commentary joins mutual urging to patience with the end of the workday; Aaron and Hur support Moses' hands until sunset so they remain steady. The scene adds physical, shared endurance through the day's limit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-432c943f5fc8e3d45957", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ء م ن; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew אמנ", "note": "The commentary's Arabic lexical gloss associates amānah with trust and firmness; Exodus describes Moses' hands as steady (emunah) until sunset. The Hebrew usage gives the root a bodily sense of sustained firmness, but the sound correspondence is unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-64e4633bea840741cf2f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Moses' hands remain steady until sunset because Aaron and Hur support them; the Hebrew faithfulness root physically joins supported endurance to an evening boundary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02d3bf2d267fa197706f", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.7.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Buyer and seller at the approaching day", "note": "When the time and day arrive, neither buyer should rejoice nor seller mourn because judgment falls on all; the ordinary logic of a profitable transaction is suspended."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e14cc1014daf3522e9b", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.7.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Silver cannot buy rescue", "note": "Silver is thrown into the streets and gold cannot deliver on the day of wrath or satisfy hunger; the commentary's capital has no use when its trading day ends."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c859a1586c2fdc596022", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God as great reward", "note": "The divine word tells Abram not to fear and promises great reward; a promised recompense precedes Abram's believing response later in the scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a08c0535ffa36fd53c0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ء م ن; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew אמנ", "note": "The commentary labels belief with the root ء م ن; Genesis says Abram trusted God and it was counted as righteousness. The Hebrew usage adds trust in a promise to the commentary's faith-and-action condition; cognacy remains unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e54a9905ef39efaa4eee", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Belief counted as righteousness", "note": "Abram believes the Lord, who counts it to him as righteousness; faith is entered as a positive item in a divine account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-afce031aff229a96d6c6", "ref": "WLC:Gen.25.33", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Birthright sold for food", "note": "Jacob has Esau swear and Esau sells his birthright; a lasting share is exchanged for immediate consumption, making a disastrous trade of one's due portion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b669074681262e439a0f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.25.34", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Birthright treated as worthless", "note": "Esau eats and drinks, leaves, and is said to have despised the birthright; the scene shows the short appetite behind the preceding loss of a lasting right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3027405df60ce06bb595", "ref": "WLC:Gen.31.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wages changed ten times", "note": "The commentary presents wage as a worker's due; Jacob tells his household that Laban deceived him and repeatedly changed his wages. The narrative gives a concrete case of an employer manipulating the account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4b1231d2e48cd63c16a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.39.23", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Joseph's prison keeper need not inspect what is entrusted to him, and the Lord prospers his work; trust, labor, and successful outcome converge with the source's secondary marketplace scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7dd9647fbc3f14c906a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.39.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Potiphar sees the Lord prosper all that Joseph does; the Hebrew success verb gives the commentary's evaluated work a narrative example without proving cognacy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf4b66058397b1e9316b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.35", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ب ر / Hebrew צבר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Joseph proposes collecting grain during seven good years for the future; the Hebrew heaping root supplies a timed economic setting for the source's measured or unmeasured heap."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3950981f7e005157d79a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.49", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ص ب ر; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew צבר", "note": "The commentary's source lexical branch calls صبرة a heap sold without measure; Genesis uses צבר for Joseph's grain piled like sea sand beyond counting. The same heap image supports a possible cognate, but the Hebrew describes storage, not an unmeasured sale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21352aeeda516b06d10a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.49", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ب ر / Hebrew צבר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Joseph heaps up grain beyond counting until counting stops; Hebrew piling resembles the source commentary's secondary heap bought without weighing, while neither text makes it patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fe47f949532621fb0176", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ء م ن; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew אמנ", "note": "The commentary makes belief one condition for escaping loss; Habakkuk says the righteous live by faithfulness (emunah) amid arrogant violence. The Hebrew context adds steadfast fidelity to belief; the table's correspondence is unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd875902f1ce7758f157", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The righteous lives by faithfulness while the proud one is not upright; the table's Hebrew firmness root adds fidelity over time to the commentary's believing exception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e11aef62ad536f85c37a", "ref": "WLC:Hag.1.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wages in a purse with holes", "note": "The commentary describes principal steadily dwindling; Haggai pictures people earning wages only to put them into a pierced purse, alongside meager harvest and food. It turns economic loss into the felt result of wasted labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2475a96f7b56eb4bb302", "ref": "WLC:Hag.1.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wages in a bag with holes", "note": "Workers earn wages only to put them in a pierced purse; the commentary's capital eaten away by passing time becomes an image of labor without retained gain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e1fda1bad5a03ae0042", "ref": "WLC:Hag.2.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Expected heap yields half measure", "note": "A person coming to a grain heap for twenty measures finds ten; the deficit is shown as a concrete count beside the commentary's diminished capital."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bcd25258375ac0cd1692", "ref": "WLC:Hos.12.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "merchant; deceitful scales", "note": "The commentary makes loss a market account and names underweighing; Hosea condemns a merchant who keeps deceitful scales and loves to oppress. It joins commercial fraud to character."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f0f6b3e6c476f945473", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The prophet condemns those who decree unjust decrees; the Hebrew prescribed-law root puts formal obligation under moral scrutiny beside the commentary's due right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ebe929cf1684b8ca5bdd", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Unjust decree deprives needy of right", "note": "The unjust decrees in the adjoining verse deprive the needy of justice and rob the poor of their right; this specifies whose due is lost when legal form becomes corrupt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-23dc24e1255c946be27e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.3.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Eat the fruit of deeds", "note": "The righteous are told that things will go well because they will eat the fruit of their own actions; it puts moral labor and due return together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-defd9c505739a44fb344", "ref": "WLC:Isa.3.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Evil deed returned upon doer", "note": "The wicked are told they will receive what their hands have done; this is the negative entry in the commentary's wage account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-794f8203477462f61b23", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Buy without money", "note": "The thirsty are invited to buy food and wine without money or price; the commentary's rescue from a losing trade likewise moves value beyond ordinary payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c72750818c7dffc38f5", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "spending and laboring without satisfaction", "note": "The commentary warns that capital can be spent without profit; Isaiah asks why people spend money on what is not bread and labor for what does not satisfy. The prophet turns failed exchange into a summons to seek what truly nourishes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5c6c91533b72e8096ec1", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Spend wages on what does not satisfy", "note": "The speaker asks why people spend money for nonbread and labor for what does not satisfy; this diagnoses a failed exchange of finite work for an empty return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d47bb1234fa095294d0", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Spend oneself for the hungry", "note": "The people are promised light if they pour themselves out for the hungry and satisfy the afflicted; the commentary's expiring personal capital is put to another's need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-41300661048bb9783048", "ref": "WLC:Isa.65.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Workers enjoy the work of their hands", "note": "People will build and inhabit and plant and eat, with days like a tree's; this reverses labor lost to another and gives the commentary's wage a durable setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2cf0ad17f8006cda1b7f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.65.23", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Labor and children not lost", "note": "The promised people will not labor in vain or bear children for calamity; enduring work and preserved family answer both forms of loss developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a9e16fdd1cbd0c09bfb9", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Recompense according to ways and deeds", "note": "God searches the heart and gives each person according to conduct and the fruit of deeds; intention and work both matter at settlement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4fd7f95ea2a0ef93676f", "ref": "WLC:Jer.22.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "neighbor's labor without pay", "note": "The commentary says the worker's wage is due; Jeremiah condemns a ruler who builds by making his neighbor work without pay. The unpaid labor is framed as injustice in the ruler's household and public power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6568c7eea6262c22c27e", "ref": "WLC:Jer.22.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Neighbor's labor without wages", "note": "The prophet condemns building a house by injustice while making a neighbor work for nothing and not giving wages; labor, rights, and payment join explicitly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-63a751b7a3cfa7285759", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Job says human days and months have appointed bounds not to be passed; the Hebrew prescription root gives a fixed due boundary to the commentary's time and payable right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0a5a549a6ecd24f6c9f", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hired laborer's day", "note": "Job asks God to look away until the mortal finishes the day like a hired worker; the comparison joins life's fixed span to an end-of-day wage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-35f55b8c3e1e0c449522", "ref": "WLC:Job.27.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ص ب ر; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew צבר", "note": "The commentary's lexical branch connects ṣubrah with a heap; Job says the wicked may heap up silver like dust. The shared heap image is specific, while the sound correspondence remains unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1ec5e89767b052f9681", "ref": "WLC:Job.27.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "others receive the wealth the hoarder prepared", "note": "The commentary says wealth cannot outlast its owner; Job says the righteous will wear the garments the wicked prepared and the innocent will divide the silver. This neighboring verse makes the transfer of accumulated wealth explicit."}

{"connection_id": "BC-fc65814513e67fa0b2b4", "ref": "WLC:Job.31.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God weighs the speaker in just scales", "note": "The commentary joins deficient scales to a final account; Job asks to be weighed in a just balance so God may know his integrity. The verse brings commercial measurement into divine judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-46683e866d9cdae66a69", "ref": "WLC:Job.31.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Integrity weighed on an honest scale", "note": "Job asks to be weighed in a just balance so God may know his integrity; the commentary's market scale becomes a test of the person rather than the goods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e320e66faf6b67492a82", "ref": "WLC:Job.7.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Life as a hired worker's term", "note": "Job compares human days on earth to the days of a hired worker; it frames the wage longing in the next verse as a figure for mortal duration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2890e3d949d0a8297fed", "ref": "WLC:Job.7.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "laborer waiting for wages", "note": "The commentary pictures the day laborer reaching the end of work and awaiting pay; Job compares human life to a hired worker longing for shade and waiting for wages. The image makes mortality itself feel like exhausting labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef8cb43127dcd5006310", "ref": "WLC:Job.7.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hired worker waiting for wage", "note": "Job compares himself to a hired worker longing for his wages; the commentary's wage at the end of the workday has a biblical sufferer's voice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9206bde14b13dd147d8e", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ص ل ح; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew צלח", "note": "The commentary labels righteous deeds صالحات; Joshua uses צלח for prospering through attention to Torah. The sound and success-related usage may illuminate the label, but the meanings differ and cognacy is unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-047a5b0c8cbfdd58c22e", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Keeping and meditating on the law day and night makes one's way prosper; the sound-corresponding Hebrew verb links success to sustained obedient conduct, though Arabic salih here means good deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-889435b27ce244eb07f4", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wages kept overnight", "note": "The commentary says a worker's wage has come due; Leviticus forbids keeping a hired worker's pay overnight. It gives the due wage a concrete deadline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1963718f307a27a1599", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hired worker's pay not held overnight", "note": "The law forbids retaining a hired worker's wages until morning; it sets an enforceable due time for the commentary's worker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1be0cc52126798a47da2", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rebuke a neighbor", "note": "The commentary's reciprocal counsel implies speaking when another is wrong; Leviticus commands rebuking a neighbor rather than silently bearing sin. It makes moral correction part of mutual responsibility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-41bbe7c4df49b78bbc2d", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.35", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "false measures and weights", "note": "The commentary describes a light scale pan and a diminished measure; Leviticus forbids falsifying measures of length, weight, or capacity. It makes fair exchange a covenantal duty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-355a9607f436b7438fe2", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.35", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Injustice in measurements", "note": "The law forbids wronging another in measuring length, weight, or quantity; the commentary's deficit on the scale is a named social wrong."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0603d89690fefd39a331", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "just balance, weights, and measures", "note": "Where the commentary contrasts a short measure with a full one, Leviticus specifies the correct balance and standard measures. The detail grounds just trade in a public rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b0c4d3d0ec6c74c559b8", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Honest scales and weights", "note": "The command supplies just scales, just weights, and honest measures; these specify what the commentary's mutual right requires at a sale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76c8320f4d8c8cac07a0", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "What profit in serving God", "note": "The speakers say service to God is vain and ask what gain comes from keeping his charge; the market question is raised within moral work itself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5e3332511a2e34f2051", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "People speak together, remembered by God", "note": "Those who fear the Lord speak with one another and a book of remembrance is written for them; mutual counsel is entered into a lasting account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-772b844ff5c0bdc466f9", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Serving God shown apart from not serving", "note": "God promises a visible distinction between the righteous and wicked, one serving him and one not; it answers the preceding dispute over the profit of service."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c767f194c136d0bfb854", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God judges wage oppressors", "note": "God comes as a witness against those who defraud hired workers of wages and wrong vulnerable people; the commentary's due wage is a matter for final judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac2b9d2603d66d8e3a52", "ref": "WLC:Mic.6.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Scant measure amid wicked treasure", "note": "The prophet identifies wicked wealth and a deficient grain measure in the house; hoarded capital and short measure share one household."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6fe7d521a2fbda3f678a", "ref": "WLC:Mic.6.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked scales and false weights", "note": "The commentary's seller who gives short measure is echoed by Micah's question about acquitting wicked scales and a bag of false weights. The prophet makes commercial fraud a test of justice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4636ff89dbd525ded5c", "ref": "WLC:Mic.6.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Can wicked scales be acquitted", "note": "The prophet asks whether dishonest scales and a bag of false weights can be counted clean; short weighing becomes a judicial question of right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-44ae1b7cf41b9eadcb4c", "ref": "WLC:Prov.10.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wrongful treasure fails, righteousness rescues", "note": "Treasures gained wickedly profit nothing while righteousness delivers from death; moral action is the profitable alternative to a full but failing purse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a7a9bb6c90c778c42eb0", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "false balance", "note": "The commentary's underweight scale is condemned directly: Proverbs calls a false balance an abomination and contrasts it with a full weight. The text adds the moral status of the market device."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70fc4c8ec64567d83aed", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "False balance and honest weight", "note": "A deceptive scale is abominable and a just weight pleasing to God; the commentary's short-weighted customer becomes an explicit moral violation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81cc2967b2721ef16828", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riches fail on the day of wrath", "note": "The commentary contrasts a losing trade with the righteous path; Proverbs says riches do not profit on the day of wrath, while righteousness delivers from death. It directly contrasts wealth with the return of right conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae31991641593c0b411f", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wealth cannot profit on judgment day", "note": "The proverb says riches do not profit in the day of wrath but righteousness rescues from death; it gives the commentary's failed trade a final accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c5a864268546697c1cb7", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ, table sound correspondence only", "note": "God delights in people who act faithfully while lying lips offend; the shared consonants connect the commentary's trust opposed to betrayal with actual conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3984cbc1d7442ed2f7b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wealth dwindling or gathering gradually", "note": "The commentary describes a trade that eats away at principal; Proverbs contrasts wealth that dwindles with wealth gathered little by little. It adds a scriptural contrast between erosion and patient accumulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e55b2b0d249a33bf1539", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wealth accumulated gradually", "note": "Wealth gained rapidly shrinks, but gathering little by little increases it; the passage counters the commentary's image of a balance steadily diminishing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b32c660d0cd6785c101", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root خ س ر; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew חסר", "note": "The commentary labels loss by خ س ر, including a deficit; Proverbs says the wicked person's belly lacks, using חסר. The specific lack-image fits, but the package offers only an unverified sound correspondence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1b9e9a09f22263137724", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic خ س ر / Hebrew חסר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The righteous eats to satisfaction while the wicked person's stomach lacks; the Hebrew deficiency verb marks a concrete moral shortfall rather than a mere sad feeling."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2facaf0fad0a548c0b61", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "just balances belong to the Lord", "note": "The commentary treats a short measure as a concrete market loss; Proverbs says just balances and weights belong to the Lord. This adds divine ownership to the duty of accurate trade."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28a651b511dc24e4bc48", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Just scales belong to God", "note": "The proverb calls a just balance and scales the Lord's and treats the weights as his work; fair commercial measurement belongs within divine order."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ae8cb9afd234800d6ce", "ref": "WLC:Prov.19.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lending to the Lord by helping the poor", "note": "The commentary says God is the buyer and guarantor of a promised return; Proverbs says whoever is generous to the poor lends to the Lord, who repays the giver. It casts care for the poor as a loan whose return comes from God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e09efcd31860d040dc6d", "ref": "WLC:Prov.19.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Lending to God by aiding the poor", "note": "The person who is gracious to the poor lends to the Lord, who repays the deed; God as counterparty and the other person's due converge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e7091b78bd4a476483a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.21.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Patient planning yields profit", "note": "The plans of the diligent lead to profit, while haste leads to poverty; this specifies how intention and duration change the trade's result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-066acd1557abd7f7058a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.22.26", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Surety for another's debt", "note": "The proverb warns against becoming security for debts; it is a contrasting practical judgment on the source commentary's secondary sabir as guarantor behind a payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-140af8c1fce3b8a8fb02", "ref": "WLC:Prov.23.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do not exhaust oneself for riches", "note": "The proverb warns against toiling to acquire wealth; it challenges the worker who gives finite strength to a capital that cannot secure life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f6289f8cf6e7da8ebfd7", "ref": "WLC:Prov.23.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wealth takes wings", "note": "The commentary rejects the belief that wealth can secure a person's future; Proverbs says wealth can suddenly make wings and fly away. It adds an image of capital disappearing beyond its owner's control."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ad5a8806d074d358ca1", "ref": "WLC:Prov.23.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wealth flies away", "note": "When one looks at riches they are gone, flying away like an eagle; it gives the commentary's vanishing earthly capital a sudden image."}

{"connection_id": "BC-81e1732facfc45dd2263", "ref": "WLC:Prov.24.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rescue those drawn toward death", "note": "The speaker commands rescue of people being taken to death; the next verse's divine weighing and repayment rests on this concrete neglected duty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f527ada8f3cea54cc87", "ref": "WLC:Prov.24.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "One who watches souls repays works", "note": "The proverb asks whether the one who weighs hearts and watches lives will not repay each according to work; divine weighing includes motives as well as output."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1e36c73d04f5e21350c2", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "uncertain tomorrow", "note": "The commentary makes each passing moment part of the capital being spent; Proverbs warns against boasting about tomorrow because one does not know what a day will bring. It grounds the deadline in uncertainty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d88f930ca8980e28e20", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Riches do not last forever", "note": "The proverb asks whether riches or a crown persist through generations; it rebukes confidence that stored money outlasts mortal time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7df72b7851ead05098a4", "ref": "WLC:Prov.28.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic خ س ر / Hebrew חסר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The person giving to the poor will not lack, while one hiding the eyes gets curses; the table's deficit root connects loss avoided with an obligation to another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b0c621a9288ae1e58fb", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do not withhold good that is due", "note": "The addressee must not withhold good from those to whom it is due when able to act; it states the commentary's mutual right as an immediate obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c18eb0335d97fcde105", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.28", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do not defer payment until tomorrow", "note": "One must not tell a neighbor to return tomorrow when the goods are in hand today; the owed portion has a deadline before time runs out."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f140849cc0da840492db", "ref": "WLC:Prov.30.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ح ق ق; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew חקק", "note": "The commentary glosses haqq as a person's right or share; Proverbs calls daily bread the speaker's prescribed portion (חֹק). The shared allotment sense is specific, but the root connection remains unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5dc24135f7f637724418", "ref": "WLC:Prov.6.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "surety for a neighbor", "note": "The commentary's lexical branch calls sabīr a guarantor standing behind a debt; Proverbs addresses someone who has become surety for a neighbor. It shows the personal risk carried by a guarantor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b55149d43c731c068141", "ref": "WLC:Prov.6.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Security pledged for a neighbor", "note": "The addressee is pictured as having guaranteed a neighbor's debt; it supplies the specific debt relation behind the commentary's lexically named guarantor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ccd382f6ce39aae2ed2", "ref": "WLC:Prov.8.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Rulers decree justice through Wisdom; the Hebrew enactment verb links the commentary's obligatory right with an enacted, just order."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1cebe10e5a2c4b040145", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The tree bears fruit in its season and prospers in all it does; the table's Hebrew root means prospering, a suggestive but unverified sound link to the commentary's salih deeds and timed reward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ec3e328758d8a1639765", "ref": "WLC:Ps.111.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Works of God's hands are truth and justice and his precepts are trustworthy; the Hebrew root ties reliability to deeds and right order rather than assent alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f274162caeb4ef469894", "ref": "WLC:Ps.111.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The precepts are established forever and done in truth and uprightness; the table's firmness family gives a lasting counterpart to transient human accounts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-424af4315ccc4d75080e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.127.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع م ل / Hebrew עמל, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Builders labor in vain if the Lord does not build; the Hebrew toil root adds dependence on God to the commentary's contrast of productive and lost work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c601f335c5392e7ea943", "ref": "WLC:Ps.127.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Early and late anxious toil", "note": "The psalm depicts rising early and sitting late to eat bread of painful toil while God gives sleep; it tests whether long hours alone secure gain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39b769e965d3054d5b3e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.15.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do right and speak truth", "note": "The person admitted to God's tent walks blamelessly, does righteousness, and speaks truth in the heart; deed and truth function together as criteria."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bdcb3ab8f71d9675a475", "ref": "WLC:Ps.34.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic خ س ر / Hebrew חסר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Even young lions lack and hunger, but seekers of the Lord lack no good thing; the Hebrew deficiency root offers a cautious lexical link to the commentary's depleted balance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-defc5bcff8207c3c3814", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.21", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Unpaid debt versus generous giving", "note": "The wicked borrow and do not repay, while the righteous are generous and give; the commentary's mutual right is tested at the point of an outstanding debt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56f72da3a18a3760a561", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Trust and do good together", "note": "The psalm joins trust in the Lord with doing good and dwelling securely; belief and beneficial action occupy one instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47b0cf4e2ad8fb3384af", "ref": "WLC:Ps.39.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Life measured in handbreadths", "note": "The speaker says God has made his days a few handbreadths and his lifetime nothing by comparison; time itself is a small measured holding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-110902ef3007417f6ef2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.39.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic root ص ب ر; package table's unverified sound correspondence to Hebrew צבר", "note": "The commentary's Arabic lexical branch calls a ṣubrah a heap sold without weighing; the psalm uses צבר for heaping up wealth and says the person does not know who will gather it. The heap becomes a warning about wealth outliving its owner; cognacy is unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48e8508a75ab53bab1dd", "ref": "WLC:Ps.39.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Someone else gathers hoarded riches", "note": "The mortal heaps up wealth without knowing who will gather it; this precisely unsettles the commentary's hoarder who expects his capital to outlast his own time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e52615da25273564f84", "ref": "WLC:Ps.73.26", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Flesh fails, divine portion endures", "note": "The speaker's flesh and heart may fail, yet God remains his portion forever; the lasting share is opposed to the exhausted mortal self and goods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f53cdfc88f2e3a3ac5b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Years consumed by toil", "note": "Psalm 90 measures the human span and says its strength is toil and trouble that soon flies away; finite time and labor are the commentary's spent capital."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af0c7be4d3bc172180a2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع م ل / Hebrew עמל, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The psalm calls the brief human span toil and trouble; Hebrew toil shares the table's consonants with the commentary's work but foregrounds its burden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a7579f86e69ede0316dc", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Counting days toward wisdom", "note": "The prayer asks to be taught to number days so as to gain a wise heart; counting the finite resource of time answers the commentary's running loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-95d18446c1824c71eec8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Establish the work of our hands", "note": "The prayer asks God twice to establish human handiwork; in a psalm of short lives, this seeks durability for labor that would otherwise vanish."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f519d9708c6e755f4db", "ref": "WLC:Zech.8.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "No wages before restoration", "note": "The prophet recalls a period with no wages for human or beast and no peace for travel; the absence of pay makes the coming restoration more than abstract prosperity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e85b36a010fe67fa7bb4", "ref": "WLC:Zech.8.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "speak truth to one's neighbor", "note": "The commentary says people counsel one another to the truth and to each person's right; Zechariah commands each person to speak truth to a neighbor and judge truthfully at the gates. It gives mutual truth-telling a civic setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ca54032967a04debfdc", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Silver weighers and traders cut off", "note": "The inhabitants are told to wail because traders are destroyed and those weighing out silver are cut off; market, balance, and final closure meet in one announcement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e7da9648c28e44da667", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Silver cannot rescue on the day", "note": "Neither silver nor gold can deliver people on the day of the Lord's wrath; monetary capital loses all purchasing power at the final deadline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d1cd7a2eb923590c9ae", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "reward of the righteous", "note": "The commentary pictures righteous workers receiving their wage; Wisdom says the righteous live forever and their reward is with the Lord. It gives the wage image an explicitly enduring outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2e3e61f5adc0414d7cc", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "what profit came from wealth and boasting", "note": "The commentary calls loss a failed account; at judgment Wisdom has the wicked ask what profit their wealth and boasting brought. It turns the market's profit question into a final moral reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34021a8861197b660672", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Profit of arrogant wealth", "note": "The condemned ask what their arrogance profited and what wealth brought them; the commentary's profit-and-loss question is spoken by those who have already lost."}
{"connection_id": "BC-812d2c4d095fa235dc2c", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wealth passes like a shadow", "note": "The condemned say all their apparent gains have gone like a shadow; the line makes the trader's disappearing capital a matter of elapsed time."}

